{"id": 3470095, "title": "Heap Buffer Over-Read via Malicious SMB Server READ_ANDX Response", "submitted": "2025-12-18", "source_url": "https://hackerone.com/reports/3470095", "excerpt": "================================================================================\nDESCRIPTION:\n================================================================================\n\n## Summary:\n\nI discovered a heap buffer over-read vulnerability in libcurl's SMB protocol \nimplementation. A malicious SMB server can send a specially crafted READ_ANDX \nresponse that causes curl to read and output up to 28KB of uninitialized heap \nmemory per request. This results in information disclosure to the attacker.\n\nThe vulnerability exists in lib/smb.c within the smb_request_state() function,\nspecifically in the SMB_DOWNLOAD state handler at lines 1110-1137.\n\nNo AI was used to discover this vulnerability. AI was used only to assist \nwith report formatting.\n\n## Technical Analysis:\n\n**Vulnerable Code (lib/smb.c lines 1116-1128):**\n\n```c\ncase SMB_DOWNLOAD:\n    if(h->status || smbc->got < sizeof(struct smb_header) + 15) {\n      req->result = CURLE_RECV_ERROR;\n      next_state = SMB_CLOSE;\n      break;\n    }\n    \n    // [1] Values read directly from attacker-controlled server response\n    len = Curl_read16_le(((const unsigned char *)msg) +\n                         sizeof(struct smb_header) + 11);\n    off = Curl_read16_le(((const unsigned char *)msg) +\n                         sizeof(struct smb_header) + 13);\n    \n    if(len > 0) {\n      // [2] Bounds check validates against smbc->got (total received bytes)\n      if(off + sizeof(unsigned int) + len > smbc->got) {\n        failf(data, \"Invalid input packet\");\n        result = CURLE_RECV_ERROR;\n      }\n      else\n        // [3] Out-of-bounds read occurs here - reads heap memory\n        result = Curl_client_write(data, CLIENTWRITE_BODY,\n                                   (char *)msg + off + sizeof(unsigned int),\n                                   len);\n```\n\n**Root Cause:**\n\n1. The attacker controls the NetBIOS header length field, allowing them to \n   claim a large message size (e.g., 0x7100 bytes)\n   \n2. The attacker pads the response with null bytes to make smbc->got match \n   the claimed size\n\n3. The attacker sets data_offset and data_length to read from any position \n   within the receive buffer\n\n4. The bounds check at [2] passes because off + 4 + len <= smbc->got\n\n5. curl reads len bytes from msg + off + 4, which includes uninitialized \n   heap memory beyond the actual SMB response data\n\n**Result:** curl outputs ~28KB when the serv", "excerpt_truncated": true, "final": "needs_human", "final_label": "Needs a human", "tone": "muted", "explain": "The evidence does not settle it either way.", "confidence": 0.7, "judge_verdict": "needs_human", "receipts_override": "", "policy_section": "", "grounding": {"verdict": "partially_grounded", "rev": "d7bde803ee83", "rev_source": "master on 2025-12-18", "counts": {"grounded": 11, "ungrounded_strong": 0, "ungrounded_medium": 2}, "problems": [{"check": "file", "status": "ungrounded", "severity": "medium", "claim": "lib/smb.c", "evidence": "lib/smb.c existed in curl's history but not at bc677a8f16a5"}, {"check": "file", "status": "ungrounded", "severity": "medium", "claim": "lib/smb.c", "evidence": "lib/smb.c existed in curl's history but not at bc677a8f16a5"}]}, "summary": "A heap buffer over-read in libcurl's SMB implementation allows a malicious server to cause curl to read and output up to 28KB of uninitialized heap memory per request.", "claims": [{"id": "C1", "kind": "flaw", "text": "A malicious SMB server can send a crafted READ_ANDX response that causes curl to read and output up to 28KB of uninitialized heap memory per request.", "status": "not verifiable", "tone": "muted", "reasoning": "The claim depends on whether smbc->got can be made larger than the actual valid data in the receive buffer msg. The provided code shows the bounds check (line 1121) and the read (line 1126), but the critical function smb_send_and_recv that populates msg and sets smbc->got is not in the provided source. Without it, we cannot confirm whether an attacker can inflate smbc->got (e.g., via NetBIOS header length) while msg contains uninitialized heap memory beyond the bytes actually received.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 1121, "note": "Bounds check uses smbc->got as the limit, but smbc->got is set in smb_send_and_recv (not provided). Cannot verify if smbc->got can exceed actual initialized data in msg.", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 1126, "note": "Read uses msg + off + 4 with length len, bounded by smbc->got per line 1121. If smbc->got accurately reflects received bytes, no over-read occurs.", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C2", "kind": "location", "text": "The vulnerability exists in lib/smb.c within the smb_request_state() function, specifically in the SMB_DOWNLOAD state handler at lines 1110-1137.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The vulnerable code location matches exactly: smb_request_state function, SMB_DOWNLOAD case at lines 1110-1137 in the provided lib/smb.c source.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 1110, "note": "SMB_DOWNLOAD case starts at line 1110", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 1116, "note": "len read from offset sizeof(struct smb_header) + 11", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 1118, "note": "off read from offset sizeof(struct smb_header) + 13", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 1121, "note": "Bounds check against smbc->got", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 1126, "note": "Curl_client_write call with computed pointer and len", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C3", "kind": "trigger", "text": "A malicious SMB server sends a specially crafted READ_ANDX response that claims a large message size and pads with null bytes to match the claimed size.", "status": "not verifiable", "tone": "muted", "reasoning": "The trigger describes attacker control of NetBIOS header length and padding to manipulate smbc->got. This logic resides in smb_send_and_recv, which is not in the provided code. Cannot verify from given source.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 1063, "note": "smb_send_and_recv called to receive msg; NetBIOS header parsing and smbc->got update happen there (not provided)", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C4", "kind": "impact", "text": "curl outputs approximately 28KB of leaked heap memory when the server only sent 256 bytes of legitimate data.", "status": "not verifiable", "tone": "muted", "reasoning": "The 28KB figure depends on C1 being true (that len can be large while reading uninitialized memory). Since C1 is not verifiable, the impact magnitude cannot be confirmed from provided code.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 1126, "note": "Output length is len, which comes from attacker-controlled response at line 1116", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C5", "kind": "reproduction", "text": "The PoC demonstrates that a server sending 256 bytes of data results in curl writing 28,672 bytes to a file, with the extra bytes being heap memory.", "status": "not verifiable", "tone": "muted", "reasoning": "The PoC scripts (run_exploit.sh, smb_exploit_server.py) are not included in the provided context. Cannot verify reproduction claims.", "citations": []}], "external_checks": [], "external_sources": [{"url": "https://sourceware.org/pipermail/cygwin-announce/2025-June/012357.html", "title": "devel/4, mingw64-x86_64-curl 8.14 - multi-protocol file transfer tool", "for": "The vulnerability exists in libcurl's SMB implementation."}, {"url": "https://sourceware.org/pipermail/cygwin-announce/2026-September/013346.html", "title": "devel/4, mingw64-x86_64-curl 8.22 - multi-protocol file transfer tool", "for": "The vulnerability exists in libcurl's SMB implementation."}, {"url": "https://sourceware.org/pipermail/cygwin/2022-October/252385.html", 